{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["Every <b>na</b> [נא] is in the sense of \"now,\" likewise: \"Oy na lanu ki chatanu\" [Woe now to us, for we have sinned]. Also: \"El na refa na lah\" [Please now heal her] — therefore HaShem answered: \"Will you not be shamed for seven days?\" \"Yumat na\" [Let him die now], \"Daber na\" [Speak now], \"Shma na\" [Hear now]. And with the addition of alef — it is the language of request. And in the language of Ishmael it is reversed, and it is like \"now\":"], :book :lamentations, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The verse quotations in parentheses are all biblical: the first from Isaiah 6:5 (here given with 'oy' rather than the biblical 'oyy' spelling), the second from Numbers 12:13, the third apparently paraphrasing God's rebuke of Miriam (the exact phrasing 'הלא תכלם שבעת ימים' does not correspond verbatim to Numbers 12:14 'ותסגר אביה שבעת ימים'), the remaining three from Numbers, Exodus, and 1 Kings respectively."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Lamentations -- Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%9E%D7%92%D7%99%D7%9C%D7%AA_%D7%90%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%94"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:07:31.107971361Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}